== D-055 in decisions.md mentions:
7:| D-055 | 2026-10-01 | delivery-orchestrator | **DG1 round-9 (F-DG1-129/213/010, all Low/non-mandatory): flip the module lint's Node-built-in and `process.*` checks from DENYLISTS to DEFAULT-DENY ALLOW-LISTS — superseding the "closed as found / exhaustive for the pinned Node" framing of D-053/D-054.** Rounds 4–8 closed loader/exec routes one at a time (`eval`/`constructor`/`Reflect` F-DG1-124; `node:process` F-DG1-125; `node:sqlite` F-DG1-127; `node:test`/`process.execve` F-DG1-128), and round 8 still surfaced `process.kill(pid,'SIGUSR1')` / `process._debugProcess(pid)` (start the in-process V8 inspector — the capability of the banned `node:inspector` — F-DG1-129 code-security and F-DG1-213 qa, the same route found independently), while F-DG1-010 showed the "exhaustive for the pinned Node 22" claim named the wrong runtime (production is Node 24; the sweep ran on Node 22). A denylist over Node's evolving surface cannot converge or be version-independent. **Decision:** `apps/api/src/architecture.testkit.ts` now **allow-lists** the built-ins and process members module source legitimately uses and **denies everything else**: `SAFE_NODE_BUILTINS = {crypto, fs, fs/promises, os, path, url, util}` (bareAllowed: a `node:` specifier is allowed only if in this set) and `SAFE_PROCESS_MEMBERS = {env, exit, argv, once}` (rule 3: `process.<member>` is a violation unless in this set). Every other built-in (module, vm, worker_threads, inspector, repl, child_process, cluster, process, sqlite, test, wasi, v8, net, http, …) and every other process member (kill, _kill, _debugProcess, execve, binding, _linkedBinding, dlopen, getBuiltinModule, abort, …) — **and anything a later Node version adds** — is denied by default. The check is therefore **version-independent** (fixes F-DG1-010's root cause: no per-Node enumeration/sweep) and structurally closes the module-IMPORT and process-MEMBER loader/exec classes version-independently (fixes F-DG1-129/213). It does NOT by itself cover a native-loader MEMBER of an allow-listed module (F-DG1-130, `crypto.setEngine`, name-banned by rule 1) nor the runtime-enumeration reach of such a member (F-DG1-132/215, residual (a)); default-deny is not a claim that no further adjacent route exists. The allow-lists were validated against the real module tree (`moduleViolations` zero): module source imports four of the seven built-ins (`crypto`, `fs`, `path`, `url`) and uses none of the four process members — the remaining entries (`fs/promises`, `os`, `util`; and `env`, `exit`, `argv`, `once`) are **reviewed safe headroom** (read/utility built-ins and read/lifecycle process members a module commonly needs), each confirmed to carry no code-loading, exec, native or debug capability, NOT claimed as current usage (F-DG1-131; a conservative allow-list, kept small). Residuals shrink to the genuinely irreducible: (a) runtime data-flow; (b) runtime code-generation then `import()` of a literal same-module path via an allowed built-in such as `node:fs` (a module may legitimately read files; a write-API-only ban is brittle); (c) `WebAssembly` global instantiation (a wasm exec, not a JS-module loader). The default-deny decides which built-in MODULES a module may import; the MEMBERS of an allow-listed module are covered by a recorded member-audit in the testkit header (F-DG1-130): the only member of the seven that loads/executes/natively-loads code is `crypto.setEngine` (a native `dlopen`), now banned by rule 1 (every spelled form; reaching it by namespace enumeration is residual (a), F-DG1-132/215). The audit was performed on Node v22.22.2 (the build-sandbox floor) and re-run by the orchestrator on Node v24.21.0 (the production target, ADR-0001) in round 11 with the identical result — `crypto.setEngine` the only native-loader member, all dangerous `process.*` members denied by default-deny, and the static lint behaving identically (it parses the AST, independent of the Node runtime); evidence `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG1/orchestrator/node24-member-audit.log`. It is re-run when the Node floor/target changes. The lint remains static defence-in-depth for the ADR-0002 module boundaries over human-reviewed code in a read-only production source tree — not a runtime security boundary. | DG1 round 8: all three reviewers PASS on candidate `sha256:e27eaf5f…`; the open items (F-DG1-010/129/213) were Low/non-mandatory and all pointed at denylist non-convergence. **Evidence:** `architecture.test.ts` 101/101 incl. default-deny positive/negative controls + the real-module-tree check (`moduleViolations` zero); the full unit suite 294/294 (no legit module import/member newly flagged); an orchestrator probe confirming process.kill/_debugProcess/getBuiltinModule, node:inspector/net/http denied and node:crypto/fs/path/url/util/os + process.env/exit/argv/once allowed; `@mth/api` typecheck/lint/prettier clean; the round-9 freeze + its three PASS reviews + auditor. | A legitimate module needs a built-in or `process` member not on an allow-list (then it is reviewed and added deliberately), or the project adopts an even stricter default-deny for the Node globals (WebAssembly, fetch). |
